C o m p i l a t i o n


      Resevoir Operation Problem
       
      Taken from:
      HW4-MoreComplicatedLinearProgram.docx
      USU CEE 6410
      Dr. Rosenberg
       
      A reservoir is designed to provide hydropower and water for irrigation. The turbine releases may
      also be used for irrigation as shown in Figure 1. At least one unit of water must be kept in the
      river each month at point A. The hydropower turbines have a capacity of 4 units of water per
      month (flows are constant during any single month), and any other releases must bypass the tur-
      bines. The size of farmed area is very large relative to the amount of irrigation water available, so
      there is no upper limit on usable irrigation water. The reservoir has a capacity of 9 units, and initial
      storage is 5 units of water. The ending storage must be equal to or greater than the beginning
      storage. The benefits per unit of water, and the estimated average inflows to the reservoir are given
      in Table 1.
       
                                      Table 1
      Month | Inflow Units | Hydropower Benefits ($/unit) | Irrigation Benefits ($/unit)
      1           2                   1.6                              1.0
      2           2                   1.7                              1.2
      3           3                   1.8                              1.9
      4           4                   1.9                              2.0
      5           3                   2.0                              2.2
      6           2                   2.0                              2.2
       
       
      Ammon Wallace
      CEE 6410
      ammon.wallace@usu.edu
  33   
  34  * 1. Define the Sets
  35  Sets
  36  t  Months in increaseing order /mon1 * mon6/
  37  loc Location in the Network /Hydro, Res, Spill, Irr, River/;
  38   
  39   
  40  * 2. Define Input Data
  41  Parameters
  42      hydropower_b(t) Hydropower Benefits Coeffiecients ($ per unit)
  43      /mon1 1.6,
  44      mon2 1.7,
  45      mon3 1.8,
  46      mon4 1.9,
  47      mon5 2.0,
  48      mon6 2.0/
  49   
  50      irrigation_b(t) Irrigation Benefits Coeffiecients ($ per unit)
  51      /mon1 1.0,
  52      mon2 1.2,
  53      mon3 1.9,
  54      mon4 2.0,
  55      mon5 2.2,
  56      mon6 2.2/
  57   
  58      inflow(t) Inflow input data (units)
  59      /mon1 2,
  60      mon2 2,
  61      mon3 3,
  62      mon4 4,
  63      mon5 3,
  64      mon6 2/
  65   
  66      max_capacity_hydro Hydropower Capacity (units per month) /4/
  67      river_minimum_flow Minimum river amount (units per month) /1/
  68      res_init_storage inital reservoir storage (units) /5/
  69      res_max_storage the max capacity of the reservoir (units) /9/;
  70   
  71  * 3. Define Variables
  72  Positive Variables
  73      X(loc, t) Decision Variables for location of water in the network for each month;
  74   
  75  Variables
  76      total_benefits the total amount of profit ($);
  77   
  78   
  79  * 4. Define equations
  80  Equations
  81      Profit Objective Function
  82      Res_capacity Maximum Reservoir Capacity
  83      River_min_capacity Minimum river capacity
  84      Turbine_capacity Maximum Turbine capacity
  85      Ending_Storage Ending storage must be greater than initial storage
  86      Res_mass_balance Mass balance equation for the reservoir
  87      Junction_mass_balance Mass balance equation for the junction;
  88   
  89  * Objective Function
  90  Profit.. total_benefits =E= sum(t, hydropower_b(t)*X("Hydro", t)) + sum(t, irrigation_b(t)*X("Irr", t));
  91   
  92   
  93  * Constrain Equations
  94  Res_capacity(t).. X("Res",t) =L= res_max_storage;
  95  River_min_capacity(t).. X("River", t) =G= river_minimum_flow;
  96  Turbine_capacity(t).. X("Hydro", t) =L= max_capacity_hydro;
  97  Ending_Storage(t).. X("Res", "mon6") =G= res_init_storage;
  98  Res_mass_balance(t).. inflow(t) - X("Hydro",t) - X("Spill",t) =E= X("Res",t) - res_init_storage$(ord(t) eq 1)  - X("Res",t-1)$(ord(t) gt 1);
  99  Junction_mass_balance(t).. X("Spill", t) + X("Hydro", t) - X("Irr", t) =E= X("River", t);
 100   
 101  * 5. Create the model using all of the defined equations
 102  Model Reservoir /all/;
 103   
 104   
 105  * 6. Solve the model and create a .lst file
 106  Solve Reservoir using LP maximing total_benefits;


